# HOW IT WORKS — The product path

File: guide/Limits.oot
User wiki. Canonical URL: https://openooda.org/guide/#Limits.oot

[Home](Home.oot) [Start](Use.oot) [Language](Language.oot) [Commands](Options.oot) [Packages](Packages.oot) [Capabilities](Safety.oot) [Pay](Pay.oot)

## 1. The model

You write `.oo` files. The compiler typechecks them. It then emits C against the `chs_rt` runtime. Host `gcc` turns that C into a native binary. You run the binary.

That is the everyday path. `ooda run` is that path.

```
.oo  →  check  →  emit C (chs_rt)  →  gcc  →  run
```

You can also ask the same frontend for other text: LLVM IR, a WebAssembly module, or standalone x86_64 assembly. Those are extra emits. They are not the everyday path.

## 2. Two binaries

`oodac` is the compiler. It checks source and emits.

`ooda` is the driver. It calls `oodac`, then `gcc`, then exec.

They are different binaries. Never copy `bin/ooda` onto `oodac/oodac`. If you overwrite `oodac` with `ooda`, check forks itself.

Put `ooda` on your `PATH` when you want the short command. Keep `oodac` as the compiler you rebuilt.

## 3. Everyday commands

Typecheck only:

```
oodac check hello.oo
```

Typecheck, emit C, compile with `gcc`, run:

```
ooda run hello.oo
```

That is `check` + emit C (`chs_rt`) + `gcc` + exec. You do not run those steps by hand unless you want to see the C.

`ooda build` stops at the binary. `ooda test` runs `verify` blocks and asserts. `ooda run` skips `verify`.

The checkout has `fixtures/hello.oo` if you want a file that already exists.

## 4. Official rebuild

You rebuild the compiler from the seed compiler:

```
cd ooda
OODAC_BIN=./bootstrap/seed/oodac bash bootstrap/oodac_pure_build oodac/main.oo oodac/oodac
OODAC_BIN=./oodac/oodac bash bootstrap/oodac_pure_build oodac/main.oo oodac/oodac
./oodac/oodac check oodac/main.oo
```

`bootstrap/oodac_pure_build` is the official rebuild. The first line uses the seed. The second uses the tree compiler. That is the one host shell entry. After that you use `ooda` and `oodac`.

The seed is a trusted `oodac`. The rebuild emits each `.oo` module to C, links once with `chs_rt`, and writes a new compiler binary.

## 5. Other emits

Same frontend. Different text.

```
oodac emit-c hello.oo
oodac emit-llvm hello.oo
oodac emit-wasm hello.oo
oodac emit-x86 hello.oo
```

`emit-c` is the everyday emit. It writes C that `chs_rt` understands.

`emit-llvm` writes LLVM IR.

`emit-wasm` writes a WebAssembly module.

`emit-x86` writes standalone x86_64 assembly.

You pick an emit when you need that artifact. The check is the same check.
LLVM as a gcc replacement is residual. Extra emits have named hello locks. They are not the everyday path.

## 6. Memory

Values drop when the last reference goes away. There is no tracing pause. The last handle frees the object.

Bump arenas live under `&AllocCap`. You create an arena, allocate from it, and reset the whole region at once. Use an arena when many short-lived values share one lifetime.

Scope locals drop when the block ends. Shared values use last-reference drop. Arenas reclaim a generation in one reset.

## 7. Capabilities

A capability is a process-local token. A function that does an effect takes the token as an argument. No token, no effect.

Prefer the small token. Take `&FsReadCap` to read a file. Take `&AllocCap` to heap-allocate. Take `&SysCap` only when you mean the broad OS.

Under `&SysCap` the runtime can apply a Linux Landlock jail and POSIX `setrlimit`. Empty allowlists and zero quotas fail closed.

HMAC attenuation derives a narrower token from a parent. The child seal is not the parent seal. A forged parent does not verify.

Measured isolate hashes the payload, the token, and the domain. You measure what you isolated. You do not get a token by guessing a string.

Caps are language tokens. The OS jail is extra confinement on Linux. Use both.

See [Capabilities](Safety.oot).

## 8. Contracts and types

Functions may declare `requires`, `ensures`, and `spec` on the header.

When those expressions are const, `oodac check` folds them at check time. A false const `requires` fails the check. You do not wait for run.

`TraitDecl` names a trait. The compiler monomorphizes: it specializes a generic body per type you actually use. You write the trait once. Check and emit see concrete types.

```
pub fn add(a: Int, b: Int) -> Int
    requires b >= 0
    ensures result >= a
{
    return a + b;
}
```

If `b` is a const that fails `requires`, check stops you.

## 9. Product std

Product std is the tree that ships with the compiler: `ooda/std`.

Import from there. That is the library the compiler knows.

A function in std that does an effect still takes a token. Std does not grant ambient authority.

## 10. Same emit, same bytes

Two `emit-c` of `fixtures/hello.oo` are byte-identical.

The compiler is a function from source to text. Same tree, same C. That is the reproducibility you hold in your hand.

Host `gcc` still applies host flags. The claim is the emit, not every downstream object file.

Official rebuild from the same seed, same sources, same emit.

## 11. Self-hosted shape

The compiler, the driver, the package tools, and the tests are `.oo`. The C you see is emit plus the small `chs_rt` runtime.

You rebuild `oodac` from `.oo` with the seed. You then use that `oodac` on your own files. That is the self-hosted loop.

## 12. How to hold it

1. Check with `oodac` or `ooda check`.
2. Run on the C path with `ooda run`.
3. Rebuild the compiler with `bootstrap/oodac_pure_build` from the seed.
4. Import `ooda/std`.
5. Pass the smallest token the effect needs.
6. Keep `ooda` and `oodac` as two binaries.

